questions.question:
Describe the pancreas:
(i) size, position and parts;
(ii) the duct system;
(iii) the histology and the islet cell types with their hormones.
State how the parts and cell types relate to the common pathologies:
(iv) diabetes mellitus: classification, pathogenesis and islet morphology;
(v) pancreatic neuroendocrine neoplasms and their syndromes.
Explain how pancreatic disease relates to distant organs and produces functional deficit:
(vi) the complications of diabetes and the secondary causes from other organs;
(vii) the syndromic associations of islet tumours.

Describe the adrenal glands:
(i) location, size and coverings;
(ii) cortex, medulla and the zones with the hormones each produces.
State how the zones and parts relate to common pathologies:
(iii) the hyperadrenal syndromes, especially Cushing syndrome;
(iv) adrenocortical hypofunction and the polyendocrine syndromes;
(v) the tumours of cortex and medulla.
Explain how disease at a distant site or in the adrenal itself produces functional deficit elsewhere:
(vi) the pituitary-adrenal relationship in Cushing syndrome;
(vii) the consequences of adrenalectomy and of pituitary hypofunction.
(viii) State the syndromic diseases that involve the adrenal glands.

Describe the skin:
(i) the layers and cell types of the epidermis;
(ii) the cell junctions;
(iii) the layers and cells of the dermis;
(iv) the appendages, nerve endings and functions.
Explain the clinical basis:
(v) why the keratin network and cell junctions matter and how blistering diseases are classified;
(vi) the systemic disorders that manifest in the skin.
Distinguish between desmosomes and hemidesmosomes, and between bullous pemphigoid and pemphigus:
(vii) in structure and target;
(viii) in the clinicopathological features of the blistering diseases.
Outline the skin tumours:
(ix) benign, premalignant and malignant epidermal tumours;
(x) melanocytic lesions.

Describe the pituitary gland:
(i) location and relations;
(ii) lobes, cell types and hormones.
Explain the clinical basis of pituitary disease:
(iii) why the anterior pituitary is vulnerable to ischaemic injury;
(iv) the other causes and the manifestations of hypopituitarism;
(v) the hormone-secreting adenomas that cause hyperpituitarism.
Distinguish between the posterior pituitary syndromes:
(vi) SIADH and diabetes insipidus;
(vii) central and nephrogenic diabetes insipidus.
(viii) State how hypothalamic disease, inflammation and hyperplasia affect the pituitary.

Describe the anatomy and histology of the breast:
(i) embryologic development and developmental anomalies;
(ii) duct system, lobules and the terminal duct lobular unit;
(iii) epithelial cell types, their immunohistochemical staining and the stroma;
(iv) hormonal influences on the breast.
Explain the clinical basis of breast lesions:
(v) why most lesions arise from the epithelial component and how common each type of lesion is;
(vi) how benign lesions are grouped by the risk of subsequent carcinoma.
Distinguish between fibroadenoma and phyllodes tumour:
(vii) in age, presentation, gross and microscopic features and genetics;
(viii) in the features that separate them and in their behaviour.

Describe the anatomy of the cervix relevant to disease:
(i) the squamocolumnar junction and the development of the transformation zone;
(ii) why the cervix is more vulnerable to human papillomavirus than the vulva or vagina.
State how the transformation zone relates to the common pathologies:
(iii) risk factors, viral types and the intraepithelial and invasive neoplasms;
(iv) the molecular action of the virus and the other cervical lesions.
Explain why cervical carcinoma spreads to distant structures and causes functional deficit:
(v) local extension;
(vi) lymphatic and haematogenous spread, with the clinical features and prevention.

Describe the intracranial haemorrhages:
(i) epidural haemorrhage: the vessel involved, cause, presentation and gross shape;
(ii) subdural haemorrhage: the vessel involved, causes and course;
(iii) subarachnoid and intracerebral haemorrhage: the space or site and the causes;
(iv) diffuse axonal injury.
Explain the pathological basis of the neurological deficit in cerebral ischaemia:
(v) global versus focal ischaemia and the vessels involved;
(vi) the types, size, territory and evolution of infarcts.

Describe the involvement of peripheral nerves in leprosy:
(i) the causative organism, route of entry and the tissues in which it grows;
(ii) the immunological basis of the polar forms and the timing of nerve damage in each;
(iii) the nerves involved, the pathological changes within them and the accompanying skin lesions.
Explain the pathological basis of the deformities:
(iv) in tuberculoid leprosy;
(v) in lepromatous leprosy.

Describe the ascending infection of the female genital tract:
(i) origin: the causative organisms;
(ii) course and the sites involved in order;
(iii) presentation and complications.
Explain the pathological basis of the sequelae:
(iv) scarring and distortion of the tubes;
(v) lymphatic obstruction and fibrosis in chlamydial and actinomycotic infection.
Outline the other lower genital tract infections:
(vi) bacterial and protozoal infections;
(vii) viral and fungal infections.

Describe tuberculous osteomyelitis:
(i) origin and routes of spread to bone;
(ii) sites of predilection and clinical presentation;
(iii) course of the infection within the spine and its relations.
Explain the pathological basis of the deformity and deficit:
(iv) in Pott's disease;
(v) in skeletal syphilis.
(vi) Distinguish tuberculous osteomyelitis from acute pyogenic haematogenous osteomyelitis.

Describe the uterus:
(i) embryologic origin and congenital anomalies;
(ii) the endometrial changes of the normal menstrual cycle.
State how the endometrium and myometrium relate to the common pathologies:
(iii) dysfunctional bleeding and inflammation;
(iv) endometriosis and adenomyosis;
(v) polyps, hyperplasia and carcinoma;
(vi) leiomyoma.
Explain why uterine disease may involve distant structures and cause functional deficit:
(vii) the sites reached by endometriosis and the deficits produced;
(viii) other distant or associated malignancies.

Describe the gross anatomy of the prostate gland:
(i) location, relations, capsule, lobes and histologic type;
(ii) the zones of the adult gland.
State how the zones relate to the common pathologies:
(iii) benign hyperplasia, carcinoma and inflammation;
(iv) the epidemiology, risk factors and histology of carcinoma.
Explain how prostatic carcinoma spreads to distant structures and the deficits it produces:
(v) local and lymphatic spread;
(vi) haematogenous spread and clinical consequences.
(vii) Outline the grading, investigation and treatment of the carcinoma.

Describe the brain herniation syndromes:
(i) definition and cause;
(ii) subfalcine herniation;
(iii) transtentorial uncal and hippocampal herniation;
(iv) central herniation;
(v) tonsillar herniation.
Explain the pathological basis of the deficits:
(vi) the factors that govern the rise in pressure;
(vii) the types of cerebral oedema and their features;
(viii) hydrocephalus.
Outline other conditions that raise intracranial pressure:
(ix) the complications of meningitis;
(x) metastatic tumours.

Describe the ovarian tumours:
(i) classification into the main groups;
(ii) age, risk factors and genetic associations.
Explain the clinical basis:
(iii) why ovarian cancers cause a disproportionate number of deaths;
(iv) the cystic lesions and polycystic ovarian syndrome;
(v) the fallopian tube disorders related to ovarian pathology.
Distinguish between serous and mucinous tumours:
(vi) in origin, morphology and behaviour;
(vii) from the endometrioid and clear cell tumours.
(viii) Outline the stromal, germ cell and metastatic tumours.

Describe goitre:
(i) definition, types and causes;
(ii) pathogenesis;
(iii) gross and microscopic morphology and complications.
Explain the clinical basis:
(iv) why patients may be euthyroid despite a goitre and why pressure symptoms arise;
(v) the features of hypothyroidism and its forms by age.
Distinguish between Graves disease and Hashimoto thyroiditis:
(vi) in pathogenesis, epidemiology and clinical behaviour;
(vii) in microscopic morphology.
Outline other thyroid disorders:
(viii) other thyroiditis and other causes of hyperthyroidism;
(ix) thyroid neoplasms and their risk factors.
